  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Careers — Senior Supervisory</div>
    <h1>How to Become a <span>Toolpusher</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">The rig's top on-site operational authority for the drilling contractor. Here's what the job actually involves, the realistic timeline from driller, and the certification that gates it.</p>
    <div class="page-meta">
      <span class="page-meta-item">Pay: <strong>$90K–$180K/yr</strong></span>
      <span class="page-meta-item">Timeline: <strong>10–15 years from entry</strong></span>
      <span class="page-meta-item">Required: <strong>IWCF Level 4 / WellSharp Supervisor</strong></span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="what-a-toolpusher-does">What a Toolpusher Does</h2>
        <p>A toolpusher oversees the entire rig for the drilling contractor — every crew, every tour, all the equipment. Where a driller runs one console for one shift, a toolpusher is accountable for the rig around the clock: scheduling crews, coordinating maintenance, managing the equipment and spare-parts supply chain, and acting as the primary point of contact between the rig and the drilling contractor's own management onshore or back at the yard.</p>
        <p>Day to day, that means walking the rig, reviewing drilling parameters and reports from each driller's tour, signing off on procedures before they execute, and being the person who gets called first when something goes wrong at 3 a.m. — not to run the console themselves, but to make the call on how the rig responds.</p>

        <h2 id="toolpusher-vs-driller">Toolpusher vs. Driller — What Actually Changes</h2>
        <p>The driller's authority is real but bounded to their own tour and their own crew. The toolpusher's authority spans every tour and every crew on the rig, plus the equipment and logistics that keep it running between wells. It's less hands-on-the-controls and more decision-and-coordination — the job shifts from executing well control procedures to approving them and managing the people who execute them.</p>

        <h2 id="the-realistic-path-to-toolpusher">The Realistic Path to Toolpusher</h2>
        <div class="career-path" style="margin-top:24px;">
          <div class="career-step">
            <h3>Driller <span class="inline-badge badge-mid">Yr 7–10</span></h3>
            <p>Run the console independently across multiple wells and formations. See the <a href="/careers/driller">full driller guide</a> for this stage.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="career-step">
            <h3>Senior / Night Driller <span class="inline-badge badge-mid">Yr 10–12</span></h3>
            <p>Take on informal coordination across tours, often acting as the toolpusher's deputy during their time off the rig. Begin IWCF Level 4 / WellSharp Supervisor training.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="career-step">
            <h3>Assistant Toolpusher <span class="inline-badge badge-senior">Yr 12–14</span></h3>
            <p>A direct understudy role on larger rigs — shadowing the toolpusher's full scope of responsibility before taking it on independently.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="career-step">
            <h3>Toolpusher <span class="inline-badge badge-senior">Yr 10–15+</span></h3>
            <p>Full operational authority over the rig for the drilling contractor. IWCF Level 4 or IADC WellSharp Supervisor required.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="the-certification-that-gates-it">The Certification That Gates It</h2>
        <p>Every operator requires supervisor-level well control certification before handing over a toolpusher's authority — <strong>IWCF Level 4</strong> or the equivalent <strong>IADC WellSharp Supervisor</strong> tier. It sits one level above the IWCF Level 3 / WellSharp Driller certification a driller needs, and it covers well control from a management and decision-approval perspective rather than hands-on execution. See the full breakdown on the <a href="/careers/certifications">certifications page</a>, or the ladder it sits on at <a href="/pillars/rig-floor-leadership">Rig Floor Leadership</a>.</p>

        <div class="note-box">
          <strong>Reviewed by a working supervisor:</strong> Jabin Travis, who <a href="/about#reviewed-by-a-working-driller">reviews our safety-critical content</a>, holds IADC WellSharp Supervisor certification. His read: the step from driller to toolpusher is less about new technical knowledge and more about trusting your own judgment under pressure across situations you can't fully rehearse — the certification proves competence, but the promotion itself is earned through a track record operators have actually watched.
        </div>

        <h2 id="what-comes-after-toolpusher">What Comes After Toolpusher</h2>
        <p>The next step up is <strong>OIM (Offshore Installation Manager)</strong> or <strong>rig manager</strong> onshore — the top operational role on the entire installation, not just the drilling contractor's piece of it. See the <a href="/careers/oim-rig-manager">OIM / Rig Manager guide</a> for what changes at that level.</p>
      </div>

